1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,920
Payday morning.

2
00:00:00,920 --> 00:00:03,830
Your phone buzzes on the desk: 15 million dong just landed in checking.

3
00:00:03,830 --> 00:00:07,890
You glance at the notification, breathe a sigh of relief, and start mapping out rent and groceries.

4
00:00:07,890 --> 00:00:12,530
Most of us picture physical bills leaving the company's bank and dropping into a private box with our name on it.

5
00:00:12,530 --> 00:00:16,800
Yet if your employer and you share the same bank, not one dong of physical cash moved across town today.

6
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:17,830
Zero bills changed hands.

7
00:00:17,830 --> 00:00:20,370
So tonight, where is your 15 million salary actually sleeping?

8
00:00:20,370 --> 00:00:26,650
Over the next few minutes, we follow one paycheck across five players: your company, the payroll bank, you, 500 coworkers, and the lending team upstairs.

9
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:28,620
It starts on your very first morning on the job.

10
00:00:29,710 --> 00:00:31,320
Think back to onboarding day.

11
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:38,500
Alongside your badge and tax papers, HR handed you one extra sheet: open a checking account at this specific bank to receive pay.

12
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:44,560
Sending money across town to other banks is technically easy; one batch file can pay dozens of banks at once.

13
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:52,900
Putting the whole company under one bank simply lowers transfer fees, cuts down on bounced wires, and leaves finance with one tidy statement to check.

14
00:00:52,900 --> 00:01:01,130
That onboarding form was message number one: your company told the bank to add another worker to payroll, and the bank opened your checking account.

15
00:01:01,130 --> 00:01:02,520
Zero cash moved that day.

16
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:07,040
But that piece of paper opened a monthly pipeline straight into the bank's books.

17
00:01:07,910 --> 00:01:08,950
Now jump to payday.

18
00:01:08,950 --> 00:01:14,890
Corporate finance uploads an encrypted batch file telling the bank to pay 15 million dong into your account.

19
00:01:14,890 --> 00:01:18,470
That file is just a digital note; it carries zero cash inside it.

20
00:01:18,470 --> 00:01:25,960
Since both the company's checking account and yours sit inside the same bank, the bank broadcasts nothing across external payment rails.

21
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:32,620
It simply opens one core ledger and changes two lines: 15 million less owed to your company, 15 million more owed to you.

22
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:34,600
Bankers call this an On-Us transfer.

23
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:37,900
Total deposits inside the bank do not move by a single dong.

24
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:39,990
Only the name of the creditor changes.

25
00:01:39,990 --> 00:01:42,580
And that text saying your account was credited?

26
00:01:42,580 --> 00:01:47,700
That reflects a Credit entry on the bank's ledger — meaning the bank now owes you 15 million.

27
00:01:48,830 --> 00:01:52,990
Meanwhile, flip over to your company's own accounting books before payday.

28
00:01:52,990 --> 00:01:59,620
To your employer, your pay was never 15 million; your gross salary is 17 million dong, booked as an operating expense.

29
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:08,170
Finance withholds 1,785,000 dong for social insurance (modeled here at 10.5% as an example) and 215,000 dong for income tax, leaving 15 million net pay.

30
00:02:08,170 --> 00:02:13,680
That 2 million still belongs to you; your company holds it briefly to pay agencies on your behalf.

31
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:18,850
When payday hits, corporate books credit Account 112 for 15 million, shrinking company cash.

32
00:02:18,850 --> 00:02:25,990
Notice the twist: your phone calls it a credit, and your employer's accountant calls it a credit, yet the word flips direction.

33
00:02:25,990 --> 00:02:28,520
On your company's books, Credit shrinks cash.

34
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:31,840
On the bank's books, Credit expands what the bank owes you.

35
00:02:32,930 --> 00:02:37,000
What if you insist on getting paid at a different bank across town?

36
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,010
Then the transfer becomes Off-Us.

37
00:02:39,010 --> 00:02:44,960
The payment instruction has to travel out through an interbank clearing switch to reach your bank.

38
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:50,910
That electronic message arrives in seconds, so your bank can credit your screen almost right away.

39
00:02:50,910 --> 00:02:57,960
Real money catches up later during central bank settlement, where banks net thousands of opposing payments together.

40
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:03,610
Only when pay goes Off-Us does corporate money actually walk out of the payroll bank's vault.

41
00:03:03,610 --> 00:03:09,810
And for your employer, every outside transfer can tack on a fee — 3,300 dong per check in our example.

42
00:03:09,810 --> 00:03:17,710
Across hundreds of workers, those tiny wire fees add up fast, which gives finance teams one more reason to keep staff on one bank.

43
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:24,650
Once salary lands, you spend it gradually across thirty days on food, utilities, and weekend plans.

44
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:31,650
Rarely does anyone yank out all 15 million on payday morning; part of every check sits idle in checking all month long.

45
00:03:31,650 --> 00:03:34,120
Bankers call these checking balances CASA.

46
00:03:34,120 --> 00:03:37,240
They pay tiny interest — 0.1% a year in this example.

47
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:42,420
Leave 10 million sitting untouched for an entire year, and you earn roughly 10,000 dong.

48
00:03:42,420 --> 00:03:47,190
This isn't financial advice; it just shows how cheap checking cash is for a bank.

49
00:03:47,190 --> 00:03:53,310
Now zoom out to the rest of your office floor: 500 coworkers also keep salary accounts at the same bank.

50
00:03:53,310 --> 00:03:59,490
If each person leaves an average of 5 million untouched, the bank sits on a steady 2.5 billion dong pool.

51
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:05,580
To a bank, 2.5 billion dong from one office payroll is gold-standard cheap funding.

52
00:04:05,580 --> 00:04:14,650
Paychecks refill every single month, staff rarely bother switching salary banks, and 500 people almost never empty their accounts on the same afternoon.

53
00:04:14,650 --> 00:04:20,200
At 0.1% interest, that entire 2.5 billion pool costs the bank barely 2.5 million dong a year.

54
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:29,030
If the bank had to raise that same 2.5 billion through 5% term deposits instead, interest costs would jump to 125 million dong — fifty times higher.

55
00:04:29,030 --> 00:04:34,100
Naturally, the bank doesn't lock your exact bills in a vault to hand to someone else.

56
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:41,150
All deposits blend into one pool, letting loan officers upstairs write 8.5% mortgages off a rock-bottom cost of funds.

57
00:04:42,110 --> 00:04:46,710
So inside the bank, who gets credit for bringing in all that cheap salary cash?

58
00:04:46,710 --> 00:04:50,200
Banks settle the score using Funds Transfer Pricing, or FTP.

59
00:04:50,200 --> 00:05:00,200
The central treasury desk acts like an internal marketplace: it buys CASA cash from retail branches at an illustrative 4.5%, then sells funding to mortgage lenders at 6.0%.

60
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:06,250
Look at how that 8.4-point gap breaks apart: retail banking pays depositors 0.1% and pockets 4.4 points.

61
00:05:06,250 --> 00:05:10,900
Treasury keeps 1.5 points to manage interest rate swings and sudden withdrawals.

62
00:05:10,900 --> 00:05:19,100
And the mortgage team charges borrowers 8.5%, pays 6%, and keeps 2.5 points — all before paying tellers, running apps, or absorbing defaults.

63
00:05:19,100 --> 00:05:25,610
Across our 2.5 billion pool, those internal spreads add up to more than 200 million dong a year in gross margin.

64
00:05:26,770 --> 00:05:29,070
Can the bank lend out all 2.5 billion dong?

65
00:05:29,070 --> 00:05:29,870
Not even close.

66
00:05:29,870 --> 00:05:36,640
Banking rules force every lender to lock away a slice of customer deposits in non-earning reserve accounts at the central bank.

67
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:45,920
Use 3% here as a clean example: on 2.5 billion dong in payroll CASA, 75 million dong stays parked at the central bank, leaving 2.425 billion dong available to fund mortgages.

68
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:49,810
For your own 10 million balance, 300,000 dong sits permanently sidelined.

69
00:05:49,810 --> 00:05:57,590
Banks earn their margin by bridging two opposite timelines: workers want instant access to every paycheck, while homebuyers need decades to repay.

70
00:05:57,590 --> 00:06:01,850
Quiet checking balances give banks the steady base they need to bridge that gap.

71
00:06:02,970 --> 00:06:07,620
What does your boss get out of delivering 500 salary accounts to one bank?

72
00:06:07,620 --> 00:06:10,950
Finance chiefs don't pick payroll banks out of habit.

73
00:06:10,950 --> 00:06:16,600
Walking into annual talks with hundreds of steady paychecks gives a company real leverage.

74
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:27,150
In exchange for that low-cost CASA flow, banks offer generous corporate sweeteners: waived wire fees, cut-rate cash management tools, and cheaper working-capital loans.

75
00:06:27,150 --> 00:06:31,730
Every contract is private, and we haven't checked any live company deals.

76
00:06:31,730 --> 00:06:40,960
You pay zero direct fee on your monthly statement, yet your resting paycheck balance is the quiet chip your company trades at the bargaining table.

77
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:45,530
You gain speed and convenience; the interest you skip is the quiet price.

78
00:06:46,460 --> 00:06:49,380
Three quick glitches can still trip up payroll day.

79
00:06:49,380 --> 00:07:01,500
First, one mistyped digit sends 15 million bouncing back to the company: corporate books reverse the payout and reopen Account 334 for 15 million until the bank number is fixed, leaving monthly expense unchanged.

80
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:10,130
Second, when payday lands on a holiday weekend, same-bank On-Us transfers often post right on time, while outside Off-Us wires wait for clearing hours.

81
00:07:10,130 --> 00:07:21,610
Third, if your employer ever ditches its payroll bank for a competitor, all 2.5 billion in cheap CASA walks out over two pay cycles — forcing the old bank to replace it with far pricier market funding.

82
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:28,200
So tonight, after payday arrives on your phone, where is your 15 million salary sleeping?

83
00:07:28,200 --> 00:07:31,450
Not in a metal safe with your name stenciled on the door.

84
00:07:31,450 --> 00:07:35,790
It sleeps inside the bank's general ledger as an unsecured debt owed to you.

85
00:07:35,790 --> 00:07:43,840
Every month you leave money sitting in checking, you act as a cheap lender to your bank, protected by deposit insurance up to statutory caps.

86
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:51,710
Next time payday pops up on screen, ask one simple question: while your balance rests there all month, who else is it quietly working for?
